 1 big thing: Diesel crisis hits schools 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Annelise Capossela/Axios The Iran war is sending diesel costs higher as many school districts are already grappling with bus driver shortages, Axios' Josephine Walker reports. 

 That's a double whammy leading to missed breakfasts, late classes and fewer school activities. The big picture: Districts are consolidating routes and cutting trips to save money and manage shortages.

 A May survey of 188 school officials found that 40% had adjusted routes, 25% cut routes and 20% limited optional trips. ⛽️ Zoom in: Cherry Creek Public Schools, in the Denver metro area, budgeted $1.5 million for fuel this year.

 It now expects to spend roughly $2 million–$2.3 million. The district is also short about 40 drivers, transportation director Mark Ingram tells Axios.

 "Every morning and afternoon, we're looking at running some routes 10–15 minutes late ... we just don't have the people to cover the routes." For some students, that can mean missing school-provided breakfast or arriving late to first period. 👩‍🏫 Peter Mannella, of the National Association for Pupil Transportation, tells Axios that districts would rather protect spending on teachers, aides and special education.

 "The inclination is always going to be: 'Let's see what we can cut out of transportation costs because we don't want to affect the school library.'" Go deeper . 

 
 
